<BlogPost
  title="Why the Same Size Fits Differently Across Brands"
  description="Fit models, ease, grading, and factory tolerance: the four hidden reasons a medium from one brand fits nothing like a medium from another."
  heading="Why the Same Size Fits Differently Across Brands"
  date="2026-07-20"
  readingTime="6 min read"
>
  <p>
    Lay three t-shirts on a bed — all labeled M, all from different brands — and measure them.
    Odds are good that no two will match: an inch of difference across the chest here, a
    shorter hem there, sleeves that end in different places on the same arm. This isn't
    sloppiness, and it isn't only the label inflation we've covered elsewhere. Two garments
    can carry identical tags, be made with identical care, and still be built for different
    bodies on purpose. Four mostly invisible decisions — who the brand fits its clothes on,
    how much room it designs in, how it scales between sizes, and how much its factories are
    allowed to vary — determine whether a garment works on you long before you touch it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Every brand fits its clothes on one specific person</h2>
  <p>
    Most clothing is developed on a <strong>fit model</strong>: a real person whose
    measurements match the brand's chosen ideal, and on whose body every sample is pinned,
    adjusted, and approved. From that process comes the brand's <strong>block</strong> — the
    base pattern from which everything else is derived. Buy from a brand and you are, in a
    real sense, borrowing its fit model's proportions: their shoulder slope, their torso
    length, the ratio of their waist to their hips.
  </p>
  <p>
    Brands choose fit models to match the customers they imagine, and those imagined customers
    differ by market and heritage. A label developed for Japanese consumers, as
    <a href="/size-guides/uniqlo-size-chart-fit-guide/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">Uniqlo</a>
    originally was, tends toward trimmer shoulders and shorter sleeves than an American
    heritage brand cut for a broader frame, even after adapting its range for international
    stores. Neither block is wrong. But if your proportions sit far from a brand's fit model,
    no size in their run will ever feel quite right — you can change the tag, not the shape.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Ease: the room the designer meant to leave</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the concept that explains most "same size, different fit" confusion:
    <strong>ease</strong>, the gap between the garment's measurements and the body it's meant
    for. A shirt for a 100&nbsp;cm chest is never 100&nbsp;cm around — unless it's meant to be
    skin-tight. How much bigger it is depends entirely on intent. A boxy overshirt might carry
    15&nbsp;cm of ease; a slim dress shirt perhaps 8; a performance base layer can have
    <strong>negative ease</strong>, cut smaller than your body so the stretch fabric
    compresses against it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Activewear makes this vivid. Leggings from
    <a href="/size-guides/lululemon-size-chart-fit-guide/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">Lululemon</a>
    are engineered to measure smaller than you and hold on; a relaxed training tee from
    <a href="/size-guides/adidas-size-chart-fit-guide/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">adidas</a>
    is engineered to float away from you. Both can be "your size" while relating to your body
    in opposite ways. When a review says a garment "runs small," it often doesn't — it just
    carries less ease than the reviewer expected, which is a disagreement about intent, not an
    error.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Grading: sizes are extrapolations, not designs</h2>
  <p>
    Brands don't design every size. They perfect one sample size — often around a women's 8 or
    a men's M — and then <strong>grade</strong> it: add or subtract fixed increments at set
    points to generate the rest of the run. Grading assumes bodies scale uniformly, and bodies
    politely decline. Going up a size adds width everywhere by formula, but real people gain
    circumference in individual places; shoulder width and torso length barely change between
    many adult sizes, while grade rules keep adding to them anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why fit complaints cluster at the ends of the size run — the sizes furthest from
    the sample carry the most accumulated extrapolation error. It's also why alpha sizing
    (S/M/L) feels blunter than numeric sizing: each letter typically spans what two numeric
    sizes would cover, so the grade steps are bigger and the "in-between" zones wider.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Tolerance: even the same garment isn't the same garment</h2>
  <p>
    Finally, manufacturing. Cut-and-sew production specs include a <strong>tolerance</strong>
    — an allowance, commonly on the order of a centimeter or two per point of measure, within
    which a garment still passes inspection. Fabric relaxes or shrinks between cutting and
    sewing, spreaders stack plies imperfectly, and two units of the identical style can land
    at opposite edges of tolerance. Stack a few of those against each other and one "medium"
    genuinely is bigger than its shelfmate.
  </p>
  <p>
    Variance multiplies with the supply chain. A retailer aggregating hundreds of third-party
    labels, like
    <a href="/size-guides/asos-size-chart-fit-guide/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">ASOS</a>,
    is really hundreds of blocks under one search bar. An ultra-fast-fashion marketplace
    sourcing from a vast rotating pool of suppliers, like
    <a href="/size-guides/shein-size-chart-fit-guide/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">Shein</a>,
    can vary noticeably from listing to listing even within one nominal size — which is why
    per-item garment measurements, where published, are worth far more there than the size
    name.
  </p>

  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">So what do you actually do with this?</h2>
  <p>
    Once you see sizing as four stacked decisions — block, ease, grade, tolerance — the
    strategy writes itself. Stop asking "what size am I?" and start asking "what are this
    garment's measurements, and what is it trying to do on my body?" Concretely:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Know your own numbers cold.</strong> Chest, waist, hip, shoulder, inseam — our
    <a href="/how-to-measure/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">measuring guide</a>
    takes about ten minutes and pays off on every purchase afterward.
    <strong>Prefer garment measurements over body size charts</strong> whenever a retailer
    publishes them, and compare against a similar garment you own that fits well.
    <strong>Learn each brand's block once</strong> — a brand's fit is far more consistent with
    itself than with anyone else, so one successful purchase teaches you the whole label.
    And <strong>read fabric content before reviews</strong>: a few percent of elastane changes
    how much ease a garment needs more than a whole size step does.
  </p>
  <p>
    The label was always the least informative thing about a garment. The block it came from,
    the room it was designed to leave, and the tolerance it shipped within tell you whether it
    will fit. Those are knowable — measurable, even — and once you start reading them, the
    three mismatched mediums on the bed stop being a mystery and start being data.
  </p>
</BlogPost>
